# Allowlist for scripts/ci/no-private-refs.sh — a security decision, not a convenience.
#
# One rule per line, `path/glob:pattern  # justification`. The glob matches the repo-relative
# path; the pattern is an ERE matched against the whole `git grep` hit (`path:line:text`).
# Blank lines and `#` comments are ignored. A justification is mandatory in review.
#
# ⛔ Do NOT add an entry to keep a real internal hostname, address or private repository path
# in a public tree. Remove it instead. An entry is only for a string that *looks* private but
# is not: a documented loopback example, a placeholder, or a term that collides with a rule.
#
# The guard and this file necessarily spell the patterns out; the guard excludes both from
# its own scan. Everything else is scanned.

# ── The product's own deny lists ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These files exist to REFUSE private hosts and addresses. The literals are the rule table —
# the thing that makes the service safe — not a reference to infrastructure that exists.
# Deleting them would delete the security behaviour, which is why they are not "removed".
src/asgi.py:routers\.internal
src/broker/egress.py:(169\.254\.|100\.100\.100\.200|192\.0\.0\.192)
src/builtin/web/scrape.py:_BLOCKED_SUFFIXES
src/sources/models.py:INTERNAL_SUFFIXES
docs/TOOL_BUILDER_BLUEPRINT.md:(Addresses|Hostnames) rejected

# ── Test fixtures asserting that refusal ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Reserved and non-resolving names used as the SUBJECT of an assertion. A real internal
# hostname would not match these patterns: they name only the synthetic fixtures.
tests/*:(blocked|x|db|git|workspace)\.internal
tests/*:(x|blocked)\.svc\.cluster\.local
tests/*:100\.(64\.0\.1|100\.100\.200)
